well all eyes are on the delta variant  which has about 150 confirmed cases here  in nevada  according to health officials on  thursday johnson and johnson reported  its vaccine to be effective  against the delta variant both moderna  and pfizer's  two-dose vaccine hold the highest  protection against the virus  unlv's assistant professor for  infectious disease  brian lubus says the delta variant is a  strain of the covet virus that is  circulating at a rapid pace  other strains will mutate over the years  he says the people should be concerned  especially those who have not been  vaccinated  the biggest difference between this  strain and the other strains is that it  spreads more easily  we haven't seen it causing a different  disease more serious disease more  hospitalizations or anything like that  it's really the person-to-person spread  that's the big difference so of course  that's going to lead to  a lot more transmission the community if  it spreads more easily  he says the only way to know if you have  the delta variant is through a  specialized test  at the state public health lab a normal  cova test will not tell you what strain  you have  he says it doesn't change how you are  treated but it's important for tracking  the variant  he added that the best way anyone can  protect themselves is by getting  vaccinated
 

